    "knowledge_point": "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/ae52c0cfa5238c68574fdc3eff16618b84ce2dfaa5846df5300bd5602b8628f5.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements is true?",
    "worked_solution": "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(-2)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(-1)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(1)=0.$\n\nTherefore , ${f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1).$",
